(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("k8s-supply-chain",{"k8s-supply-chain/the-software-supply-chain-threat-model-and-slsa":{"html":"\u003cp>Most cluster compromises start before the cluster: in a dependency, a build server or a registry. A supply chain threat model lists every step from commit to container and what can go wrong at each.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The stages from source to running pod\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Typical attacks at each stage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What SLSA levels ask for\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Provenance and why it matters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing the first controls\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-stages\">The stages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Code is written and reviewed, dependencies are pulled, a build runs, an image is pushed to a registry, a deployment tool pulls it and the cluster runs it. Each hand over is a place where something can be swapped, so each needs a check.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"typical-attacks\">Typical attacks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Examples include a malicious or hijacked dependency, a stolen CI token that pushes a poisoned image, a tag that is moved to a different image, a compromised build runner and an unreviewed change merged by a bot. Attackers prefer places with weak access control and little logging.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"slsa-levels\">SLSA levels\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>SLSA, the supply chain levels for software artifacts, describes how trustworthy a build is. Level one asks for provenance that records how an artifact was built. Higher levels ask for builds on a hosted service that signs provenance, with isolation so a user cannot forge it. The levels give a roadmap instead of a single yes or no.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"provenance\">Provenance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Provenance is a signed statement: this image digest was built from this commit by this pipeline at this time. A cluster that checks provenance can refuse an image built by an unknown pipeline even if the registry contains it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"first-controls\">First controls\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pin images by digest, scan dependencies, require reviews, protect the main branch and use short lived credentials in CI. Then sign images and verify at admission. Start with the steps that block the most likely attack for you.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"src&gt;build;build&gt;reg;reg&gt;cl\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The software supply chain threat model and SLSA\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"src\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Source and dependencies\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"build\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Build pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"reg\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Registry\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cl\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cluster\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Source and dependencies to Build pipeline; 2, Build pipeline to Registry; 3, Registry to Cluster.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"src\">\u003cdt>Source and dependencies \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Code and packages.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hijacked package.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"build\">\u003cdt>Build pipeline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Produces the image.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stolen token.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"reg\">\u003cdt>Registry \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores images.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Moved tag.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cl\">\u003cdt>Cluster \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Runs the image.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unverified image.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-tag-that-changed-after-review\">Incident: The tag that changed after review\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deployment pulled a latest tag that pointed to an image nobody had reviewed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pod web-1 -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].imageID}'\ncrane digest registry.example.com/web:1.8\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pod web-1 -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.status.containerStatuses[0].imageID}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">registry.example.com/web@sha256:9f2a77c1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>crane digest registry.example.com/web:1.8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sha256:41be0d20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The tag 1.8 had been moved to a new image after the review, so the running digest did not match the reviewed one. The team pinned deployments by digest, made tags immutable in the registry and added a check that compares the deployed digest to the one in the release record.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Deploying mutable tags such as latest\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long lived registry credentials in CI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting any image in the registry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No record of what built an image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Starting with signing before basic access control\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-stages","text":"The stages"},{"id":"typical-attacks","text":"Typical attacks"},{"id":"slsa-levels","text":"SLSA levels"},{"id":"provenance","text":"Provenance"},{"id":"first-controls","text":"First controls"},{"id":"incident-the-tag-that-changed-after-review","text":"Incident: The tag that changed after review"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SLSA specification","u":"https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.0/"},{"t":"Supply chain security paper","u":"https://github.com/cncf/tag-security/tree/main/community/working-groups/supply-chain-security"},{"t":"Kubernetes security overview","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/"}],"text":"Most cluster compromises start before the cluster: in a dependency, a build server or a registry. A supply chain threat model lists every step from commit to container and what can go wrong at each. What you will learn The stages from source to running pod Typical attacks at each stage What SLSA levels ask for Provenance and why it matters Choosing the first controls The stages Code is written and reviewed, dependencies are pulled, a build runs, an image is pushed to a registry, a deployment tool pulls it and the cluster runs it. Each hand over is a place where something can be swapped, so each needs a check. Typical attacks Examples include a malicious or hijacked dependency, a stolen CI token that pushes a poisoned image, a tag that is moved to a different image, a compromised build runner and an unreviewed change merged by a bot. Attackers prefer places with weak access control and little logging. SLSA levels SLSA, the supply chain levels for software artifacts, describes how trustworthy a build is. Level one asks for provenance that records how an artifact was built. Higher levels ask for builds on a hosted service that signs provenance, with isolation so a user cannot forge it. The levels give a roadmap instead of a single yes or no. Provenance Provenance is a signed statement: this image digest was built from this commit by this pipeline at this time. A cluster that checks provenance can refuse an image built by an unknown pipeline even if the registry contains it. First controls Pin images by digest, scan dependencies, require reviews, protect the main branch and use short lived credentials in CI. Then sign images and verify at admission. Start with the steps that block the most likely attack for you. The software supply chain threat model and SLSA Flow in order: 1, Source and dependencies to Build pipeline; 2, Build pipeline to Registry; 3, Registry to Cluster. Outside party Step Data store Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Source and dependencies Outside party What it does: Code and packages. If it fails or is weak: Hijacked package. Build pipeline Step What it does: Produces the image. If it fails or is weak: Stolen token. Registry Data store What it does: Stores images. If it fails or is weak: Moved tag. Cluster Security What it does: Runs the image. If it fails or is weak: Unverified image. Incident: The tag that changed after review Incident A deployment pulled a latest tag that pointed to an image nobody had reviewed. $ kubectl get pod web-1 -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.status.containerStatuses[0].imageID}&#x27; registry.example.com/web@sha256:9f2a77c1 $ crane digest registry.example.com/web:1.8 sha256:41be0d20 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The tag 1.8 had been moved to a new image after the review, so the running digest did not match the reviewed one. The team pinned deployments by digest, made tags immutable in the registry and added a check that compares the deployed digest to the one in the release record. Common mistakes Deploying mutable tags such as latest Long lived registry credentials in CI Trusting any image in the registry No record of what built an image Starting with signing before basic access control"},"k8s-supply-chain/image-scanning-and-vulnerability-management":{"html":"\u003cp>A scanner reports hundreds of findings for almost any image. The skill is to cut noise, decide what to fix first and keep a record of what you accepted and why.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What scanners check and what they miss\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reducing findings with small, current base images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prioritising by severity, exploitability and exposure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Gates in CI and continuous rescans\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Managing exceptions with VEX and expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-scanners-check\">What scanners check\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tools such as Trivy compare package versions in the image to vulnerability databases. They also flag misconfigurations and secrets in files. They do not find unknown flaws or logic errors, and a clean result today may not be clean next week as new vulnerabilities are published.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"smaller-images\">Smaller images\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most findings come from the operating system packages in the base image. A minimal or distroless base, multi-stage builds and regular rebuilds remove many findings without any code change. Fewer packages also mean a smaller attack surface.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prioritising\">Prioritising\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Severity alone is not enough. Ask whether the vulnerable package is loaded, whether a fix exists, whether a public exploit is known and whether the workload is reachable from the internet. A critical issue in an unused tool in a build image matters less than a high severity issue in an exposed service.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"gates-and-rescans\">Gates and rescans\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Fail a pipeline on fixable critical and high findings that apply to the runtime image. Rescan images already in the registry on a schedule, because new vulnerabilities appear after the build. Tell owners through tickets with the image and the fix version.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exceptions\">Exceptions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When you cannot fix something, record the reason, the owner and an expiry date. A VEX statement can state that a vulnerability is not exploitable in this product. Reviewing expiring exceptions keeps the list honest.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"ci&gt;reg;reg&gt;tri;tri&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Image scanning and vulnerability management\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ci\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CI scan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"reg\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Registry rescan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tri\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Triage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rebuild and deploy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, CI scan to Registry rescan; 2, Registry rescan to Triage; 3, Triage to Rebuild and deploy.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ci\">\u003cdt>CI scan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Gates the build.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignored failures.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"reg\">\u003cdt>Registry rescan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Finds new issues.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No owner.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tri\">\u003cdt>Triage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Prioritise and assign.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Severity only.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Rebuild and deploy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ships the fix.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow rollout.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-critical-finding-two-weeks-after-release\">Incident: A critical finding two weeks after release\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A library vulnerability was published after the image shipped and nobody noticed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --ignore-unfixed registry.example.com/api@sha256:41be0d20\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --ignore-unfixed registry.example.com/api@sha256:41be0d20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Total: 2 (HIGH: 1, CRITICAL: 1)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">libssl3  CVE-2026-0001  CRITICAL  3.0.11-1  3.0.13-1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The image had no scheduled rescan, so the new finding only appeared during a manual check. A nightly rescan job now opens a ticket for the owning team, and the base image is rebuilt weekly so most fixes arrive without effort.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Failing builds on every low severity finding\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scanning only at build time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring whether a fix exists\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exceptions with no expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Big images with shells and package managers\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-scanners-check","text":"What scanners check"},{"id":"smaller-images","text":"Smaller images"},{"id":"prioritising","text":"Prioritising"},{"id":"gates-and-rescans","text":"Gates and rescans"},{"id":"exceptions","text":"Exceptions"},{"id":"incident-a-critical-finding-two-weeks-after-release","text":"Incident: A critical finding two weeks after release"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Trivy","u":"https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy"},{"t":"Kubernetes security overview","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/"},{"t":"SLSA specification","u":"https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.0/"}],"text":"A scanner reports hundreds of findings for almost any image. The skill is to cut noise, decide what to fix first and keep a record of what you accepted and why. What you will learn What scanners check and what they miss Reducing findings with small, current base images Prioritising by severity, exploitability and exposure Gates in CI and continuous rescans Managing exceptions with VEX and expiry What scanners check Tools such as Trivy compare package versions in the image to vulnerability databases. They also flag misconfigurations and secrets in files. They do not find unknown flaws or logic errors, and a clean result today may not be clean next week as new vulnerabilities are published. Smaller images Most findings come from the operating system packages in the base image. A minimal or distroless base, multi-stage builds and regular rebuilds remove many findings without any code change. Fewer packages also mean a smaller attack surface. Prioritising Severity alone is not enough. Ask whether the vulnerable package is loaded, whether a fix exists, whether a public exploit is known and whether the workload is reachable from the internet. A critical issue in an unused tool in a build image matters less than a high severity issue in an exposed service. Gates and rescans Fail a pipeline on fixable critical and high findings that apply to the runtime image. Rescan images already in the registry on a schedule, because new vulnerabilities appear after the build. Tell owners through tickets with the image and the fix version. Exceptions When you cannot fix something, record the reason, the owner and an expiry date. A VEX statement can state that a vulnerability is not exploitable in this product. Reviewing expiring exceptions keeps the list honest. Image scanning and vulnerability management Flow in order: 1, CI scan to Registry rescan; 2, Registry rescan to Triage; 3, Triage to Rebuild and deploy. Step Observability Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. CI scan Step What it does: Gates the build. If it fails or is weak: Ignored failures. Registry rescan Observability What it does: Finds new issues. If it fails or is weak: No owner. Triage Step What it does: Prioritise and assign. If it fails or is weak: Severity only. Rebuild and deploy Service What it does: Ships the fix. If it fails or is weak: Slow rollout. Incident: A critical finding two weeks after release Incident A library vulnerability was published after the image shipped and nobody noticed. $ trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --ignore-unfixed registry.example.com/api@sha256:41be0d20 Total: 2 (HIGH: 1, CRITICAL: 1) libssl3 CVE-2026-0001 CRITICAL 3.0.11-1 3.0.13-1 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The image had no scheduled rescan, so the new finding only appeared during a manual check. A nightly rescan job now opens a ticket for the owning team, and the base image is rebuilt weekly so most fixes arrive without effort. Common mistakes Failing builds on every low severity finding Scanning only at build time Ignoring whether a fix exists Exceptions with no expiry Big images with shells and package managers"},"k8s-supply-chain/sboms-signing-and-verification-with-cosign":{"html":"\u003cp>A signature says who vouched for an image. A software bill of materials says what is inside it. Together they let you answer which images contain a bad library and prove who built them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What an SBOM contains and how to use it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Signing images and attaching attestations\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keyless signing with identity and a transparency log\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Verifying signatures in CI and in the cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Key and identity management\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sboms\">SBOMs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An SBOM lists the packages and versions in an image, in formats such as SPDX or CycloneDX. Generate it at build time, store it with the image and search it when a new vulnerability is announced. You can then list affected images in minutes instead of rescanning everything.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"signing\">Signing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cosign signs the image digest and stores the signature next to the image in the registry. An attestation is a signed statement about the image, such as its SBOM or its provenance. Sign the digest, never the tag, since a tag can move.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keyless-signing\">Keyless signing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keyless signing uses a short lived certificate issued to the identity of the CI job, such as a workflow in a repository, through OpenID Connect. The signing event is recorded in a public transparency log. You do not store a long lived key, and a verifier checks the identity and the issuer instead of a key.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verifying\">Verifying\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Verification checks that a signature exists for the digest and that the signer identity and issuer match what you expect. Do it in the pipeline before promotion and again in the cluster at admission. A signature from the wrong identity must fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"managing-identities\">Managing identities\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If you use keys, keep them in a KMS or hardware module and rotate them. With keyless signing, protect the CI workflow itself, because whoever can run it can sign. Restrict which branches and workflows are allowed to produce releases.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"b&gt;s;s&gt;r;r&gt;v\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">SBOMs, signing and verification with cosign\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"b\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Build job\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"s\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">cosign sign\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"r\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Registry\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"v\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Verify step\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Build job to cosign sign; 2, cosign sign to Registry; 3, Registry to Verify step.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"b\">\u003cdt>Build job \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Builds and generates SBOM.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Open to any branch.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"s\">\u003cdt>cosign sign \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Signs digest.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong identity.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"r\">\u003cdt>Registry \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Image and signature.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Tag moved.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"v\">\u003cdt>Verify step \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checks identity.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not enforced.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-signed-image-from-the-wrong-workflow\">Incident: A signed image from the wrong workflow\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An image signed by a test workflow was promoted to production.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cosign verify registry.example.com/api@sha256:41be0d20 --certificate-identity-regexp 'https://github.com/example/.*' --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com | head -c 120\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>cosign verify registry.example.com/api@sha256:41be0d20 --certificate-identity-regexp \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;https://github.com/example/.*&#x27;\u003c/span> --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 120\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Verification for registry.example.com/api@sha256:41be0d20 --\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">The following checks were performed on each of these signatures:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The identity pattern accepted any workflow in the organisation, including a test workflow. The policy was tightened to the release workflow on the main branch, and the verification step now fails for any other identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Signing tags instead of digests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Accepting any identity from the right issuer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No SBOM stored for later search\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long lived signing keys in CI variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Verifying only in CI and never at admission\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"sboms","text":"SBOMs"},{"id":"signing","text":"Signing"},{"id":"keyless-signing","text":"Keyless signing"},{"id":"verifying","text":"Verifying"},{"id":"managing-identities","text":"Managing identities"},{"id":"incident-a-signed-image-from-the-wrong-workflow","text":"Incident: A signed image from the wrong workflow"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Sigstore documentation","u":"https://docs.sigstore.dev/"},{"t":"Cosign","u":"https://github.com/sigstore/cosign"},{"t":"Syft SBOM generator","u":"https://github.com/anchore/syft"}],"text":"A signature says who vouched for an image. A software bill of materials says what is inside it. Together they let you answer which images contain a bad library and prove who built them. What you will learn What an SBOM contains and how to use it Signing images and attaching attestations Keyless signing with identity and a transparency log Verifying signatures in CI and in the cluster Key and identity management SBOMs An SBOM lists the packages and versions in an image, in formats such as SPDX or CycloneDX. Generate it at build time, store it with the image and search it when a new vulnerability is announced. You can then list affected images in minutes instead of rescanning everything. Signing Cosign signs the image digest and stores the signature next to the image in the registry. An attestation is a signed statement about the image, such as its SBOM or its provenance. Sign the digest, never the tag, since a tag can move. Keyless signing Keyless signing uses a short lived certificate issued to the identity of the CI job, such as a workflow in a repository, through OpenID Connect. The signing event is recorded in a public transparency log. You do not store a long lived key, and a verifier checks the identity and the issuer instead of a key. Verifying Verification checks that a signature exists for the digest and that the signer identity and issuer match what you expect. Do it in the pipeline before promotion and again in the cluster at admission. A signature from the wrong identity must fail. Managing identities If you use keys, keep them in a KMS or hardware module and rotate them. With keyless signing, protect the CI workflow itself, because whoever can run it can sign. Restrict which branches and workflows are allowed to produce releases. SBOMs, signing and verification with cosign Flow in order: 1, Build job to cosign sign; 2, cosign sign to Registry; 3, Registry to Verify step. Step Security Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Build job Step What it does: Builds and generates SBOM. If it fails or is weak: Open to any branch. cosign sign Security What it does: Signs digest. If it fails or is weak: Wrong identity. Registry Data store What it does: Image and signature. If it fails or is weak: Tag moved. Verify step Security What it does: Checks identity. If it fails or is weak: Not enforced. Incident: A signed image from the wrong workflow Incident An image signed by a test workflow was promoted to production. $ cosign verify registry.example.com/api@sha256:41be0d20 --certificate-identity-regexp &#x27;https://github.com/example/.*&#x27; --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com | head -c 120 Verification for registry.example.com/api@sha256:41be0d20 -- The following checks were performed on each of these signatures: (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The identity pattern accepted any workflow in the organisation, including a test workflow. The policy was tightened to the release workflow on the main branch, and the verification step now fails for any other identity. Common mistakes Signing tags instead of digests Accepting any identity from the right issuer No SBOM stored for later search Long lived signing keys in CI variables Verifying only in CI and never at admission"},"k8s-supply-chain/admission-enforcement-of-provenance-with-policy":{"html":"\u003cp>Verification in CI is advisory until the cluster refuses anything that skipped it. Admission policy turns your supply chain rules into a gate that every workload must pass.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What an image policy checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Kyverno and similar engines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Audit mode before enforce\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exceptions and break glass access\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Availability of the policy engine itself\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-check\">What to check\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Useful rules include: images must come from approved registries, must be referenced by digest, must have a valid signature from the release identity and must have an SBOM or provenance attestation. Keep rules small and clear so teams can understand a rejection.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"engines\">Engines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Kyverno expresses policies as Kubernetes resources and has built in image verification. Other options are OPA Gatekeeper, the built in ValidatingAdmissionPolicy for simple rules and the Sigstore policy controller. Choose by the team skills and by which checks you need, such as signature verification.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"audit-first\">Audit first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start in audit mode, which records violations without blocking. Review the report, fix or exempt each case, then switch namespaces to enforce one by one. A sudden global enforce is the quickest way to stop deployments across the company.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exceptions\">Exceptions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some system namespaces and vendor images need exceptions. Write them as explicit, reviewed rules with an owner and expiry. Provide a break glass path, such as an annotated exemption with an audit trail, for real emergencies.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"engine-availability\">Engine availability\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A policy webhook with failure policy fail blocks changes when it is down, and with ignore it lets everything through. Run several replicas, spread them across zones, exclude the policy namespace and alert on webhook latency and errors. Decide the failure mode on purpose.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"dev&gt;api;api&gt;pol;pol&gt;res\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Admission enforcement of provenance with policy\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dev\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Deploy request\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy engine\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"res\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Allow or deny\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Deploy request to API server; 2, API server to Policy engine; 3, Policy engine to Allow or deny.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dev\">\u003cdt>Deploy request \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Applies a pod.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unsigned image.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Calls the webhook.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Webhook timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Policy engine \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Verifies signature.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Down or slow.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"res\">\u003cdt>Allow or deny \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Returns the decision.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong failure mode.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-enforce-mode-that-blocked-a-security-patch\">Incident: Enforce mode that blocked a security patch\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A fix could not be deployed because its new image was not signed yet.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml\nkubectl get cpol verify-images -o jsonpath='{.spec.validationFailureAction}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error from server: admission webhook &quot;verify-images.kyverno.svc&quot; denied the request: image registry.example.com/api@sha256:77aa verification failed: no signatures found\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get cpol verify-images -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.validationFailureAction}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Enforce\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The build pipeline had signed images only on the main branch, but the patch came from a release branch. The pipeline was fixed to sign release branches, and the team used a time limited exception to ship the urgent patch while the fix was in review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Switching to enforce without an audit period\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rules nobody can read or explain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No exception process for emergencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running one policy engine replica\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not excluding the policy engine own namespace\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-to-check","text":"What to check"},{"id":"engines","text":"Engines"},{"id":"audit-first","text":"Audit first"},{"id":"exceptions","text":"Exceptions"},{"id":"engine-availability","text":"Engine availability"},{"id":"incident-enforce-mode-that-blocked-a-security-patch","text":"Incident: Enforce mode that blocked a security patch"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kyverno documentation","u":"https://kyverno.io/docs/"},{"t":"Validating admission policy","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/validating-admission-policy/"},{"t":"Admission controllers","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/admission-controllers/"}],"text":"Verification in CI is advisory until the cluster refuses anything that skipped it. Admission policy turns your supply chain rules into a gate that every workload must pass. What you will learn What an image policy checks Kyverno and similar engines Audit mode before enforce Exceptions and break glass access Availability of the policy engine itself What to check Useful rules include: images must come from approved registries, must be referenced by digest, must have a valid signature from the release identity and must have an SBOM or provenance attestation. Keep rules small and clear so teams can understand a rejection. Engines Kyverno expresses policies as Kubernetes resources and has built in image verification. Other options are OPA Gatekeeper, the built in ValidatingAdmissionPolicy for simple rules and the Sigstore policy controller. Choose by the team skills and by which checks you need, such as signature verification. Audit first Start in audit mode, which records violations without blocking. Review the report, fix or exempt each case, then switch namespaces to enforce one by one. A sudden global enforce is the quickest way to stop deployments across the company. Exceptions Some system namespaces and vendor images need exceptions. Write them as explicit, reviewed rules with an owner and expiry. Provide a break glass path, such as an annotated exemption with an audit trail, for real emergencies. Engine availability A policy webhook with failure policy fail blocks changes when it is down, and with ignore it lets everything through. Run several replicas, spread them across zones, exclude the policy namespace and alert on webhook latency and errors. Decide the failure mode on purpose. Admission enforcement of provenance with policy Flow in order: 1, Deploy request to API server; 2, API server to Policy engine; 3, Policy engine to Allow or deny. Outside party Service Security Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Deploy request Outside party What it does: Applies a pod. If it fails or is weak: Unsigned image. API server Service What it does: Calls the webhook. If it fails or is weak: Webhook timeout. Policy engine Security What it does: Verifies signature. If it fails or is weak: Down or slow. Allow or deny Step What it does: Returns the decision. If it fails or is weak: Wrong failure mode. Incident: Enforce mode that blocked a security patch Incident A fix could not be deployed because its new image was not signed yet. $ kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml Error from server: admission webhook \"verify-images.kyverno.svc\" denied the request: image registry.example.com/api@sha256:77aa verification failed: no signatures found $ kubectl get cpol verify-images -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.validationFailureAction}&#x27; Enforce (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The build pipeline had signed images only on the main branch, but the patch came from a release branch. The pipeline was fixed to sign release branches, and the team used a time limited exception to ship the urgent patch while the fix was in review. Common mistakes Switching to enforce without an audit period Rules nobody can read or explain No exception process for emergencies Running one policy engine replica Not excluding the policy engine own namespace"},"k8s-supply-chain/runtime-detection-with-falco-and-audit-events":{"html":"\u003cp>Prevention fails sometimes. Runtime detection watches what containers do and what is done through the API, so unusual behaviour such as a shell in a production pod raises an alert.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What Falco observes and how rules work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Useful default detections\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Kubernetes audit events as a second source\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tuning rules to reduce false alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alert routing and response\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-falco-works\">How Falco works\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Falco watches system calls through an eBPF probe or a kernel module and matches them against rules. A rule has a condition, an output message and a priority. Because it sees real behaviour, it catches what static scans cannot, such as a process opening a reverse shell.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"useful-detections\">Useful detections\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with rules for a shell started inside a container, a write below system directories, reading of service account tokens by unexpected processes, outbound connections to unusual ports and use of package managers in running containers. These are rare in healthy workloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"audit-events\">Audit events\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The API server audit log records who did what through the API: secret reads, exec into pods, role changes. Rules or a log pipeline can alert on a spike of secret reads or on a binding to cluster admin. Together with Falco this covers both inside and outside the container.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tuning\">Tuning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every cluster has legitimate shells and package managers, such as debug sessions or build jobs. Add exceptions by namespace, image or process name rather than disabling a rule. Track the share of alerts that were real and revisit the noisy ones weekly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"routing-and-response\">Routing and response\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Send alerts with the pod, namespace, image and rule name to a channel or ticket system. Define what to do for each priority: for example a shell in production triggers quarantine through a network policy label and a review of the audit log.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"k&gt;f;f&gt;a;a&gt;r\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Runtime detection with Falco and audit events\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"k\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Kernel events\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"f\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Falco rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"a\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Alert pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"r\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Responder\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Kernel events to Falco rules; 2, Falco rules to Alert pipeline; 3, Alert pipeline to Responder.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"k\">\u003cdt>Kernel events \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> System calls.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Probe missing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"f\">\u003cdt>Falco rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Match behaviour.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Noisy rules.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"a\">\u003cdt>Alert pipeline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Adds context, routes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No owner.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"r\">\u003cdt>Responder \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Contains and investigates.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Late response.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-shell-in-the-payments-pod\">Incident: A shell in the payments pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Falco reported a shell started in a production container at night.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs -n falco ds/falco | grep payments | head -2\nkubectl get events -n payments --field-selector involvedObject.name=payments-7d9 | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs -n falco ds/falco | grep payments | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;priority&quot;:&quot;Warning&quot;,&quot;rule&quot;:&quot;Terminal shell in container&quot;,&quot;output&quot;:&quot;A shell was spawned in a container (user=root pod=payments-7d9 image=registry.example.com/payments:2.4 command=sh -c id)&quot;}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get events -n payments --field-selector involvedObject.name=payments-7d9 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The shell ran from a vulnerable endpoint that accepted a command. The pod was quarantined with a deny network policy, evidence was copied, the image was patched and a rule for outbound connections from this namespace was added. The alert reached the responder within a minute because it carried the pod and image names.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Disabling a noisy rule instead of adding a scoped exception\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerts with no context\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No plan for what a responder does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relying only on image scans\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not enabling audit logging on the API server\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"how-falco-works","text":"How Falco works"},{"id":"useful-detections","text":"Useful detections"},{"id":"audit-events","text":"Audit events"},{"id":"tuning","text":"Tuning"},{"id":"routing-and-response","text":"Routing and response"},{"id":"incident-a-shell-in-the-payments-pod","text":"Incident: A shell in the payments pod"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Falco documentation","u":"https://falco.org/docs/"},{"t":"Auditing","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/audit/"},{"t":"Kubernetes security overview","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/"}],"text":"Prevention fails sometimes. Runtime detection watches what containers do and what is done through the API, so unusual behaviour such as a shell in a production pod raises an alert. What you will learn What Falco observes and how rules work Useful default detections Kubernetes audit events as a second source Tuning rules to reduce false alerts Alert routing and response How Falco works Falco watches system calls through an eBPF probe or a kernel module and matches them against rules. A rule has a condition, an output message and a priority. Because it sees real behaviour, it catches what static scans cannot, such as a process opening a reverse shell. Useful detections Start with rules for a shell started inside a container, a write below system directories, reading of service account tokens by unexpected processes, outbound connections to unusual ports and use of package managers in running containers. These are rare in healthy workloads. Audit events The API server audit log records who did what through the API: secret reads, exec into pods, role changes. Rules or a log pipeline can alert on a spike of secret reads or on a binding to cluster admin. Together with Falco this covers both inside and outside the container. Tuning Every cluster has legitimate shells and package managers, such as debug sessions or build jobs. Add exceptions by namespace, image or process name rather than disabling a rule. Track the share of alerts that were real and revisit the noisy ones weekly. Routing and response Send alerts with the pod, namespace, image and rule name to a channel or ticket system. Define what to do for each priority: for example a shell in production triggers quarantine through a network policy label and a review of the audit log. Runtime detection with Falco and audit events Flow in order: 1, Kernel events to Falco rules; 2, Falco rules to Alert pipeline; 3, Alert pipeline to Responder. Observability Security Step Outside party Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Kernel events Observability What it does: System calls. If it fails or is weak: Probe missing. Falco rules Security What it does: Match behaviour. If it fails or is weak: Noisy rules. Alert pipeline Step What it does: Adds context, routes. If it fails or is weak: No owner. Responder Outside party What it does: Contains and investigates. If it fails or is weak: Late response. Incident: A shell in the payments pod Incident Falco reported a shell started in a production container at night. $ kubectl logs -n falco ds/falco | grep payments | head -2 {\"priority\":\"Warning\",\"rule\":\"Terminal shell in container\",\"output\":\"A shell was spawned in a container (user=root pod=payments-7d9 image=registry.example.com/payments:2.4 command=sh -c id)\"} $ kubectl get events -n payments --field-selector involvedObject.name=payments-7d9 | tail -2 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The shell ran from a vulnerable endpoint that accepted a command. The pod was quarantined with a deny network policy, evidence was copied, the image was patched and a rule for outbound connections from this namespace was added. The alert reached the responder within a minute because it carried the pod and image names. Common mistakes Disabling a noisy rule instead of adding a scoped exception Alerts with no context No plan for what a responder does Relying only on image scans Not enabling audit logging on the API server"},"k8s-supply-chain/sandboxing-seccomp-apparmor-and-runtime-classes":{"html":"\u003cp>Containers share the host kernel, so a flaw in the kernel can become an escape. Syscall filters and sandboxed runtimes shrink the exposed kernel surface for workloads that need it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The shared kernel risk\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Seccomp profiles and RuntimeDefault\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>AppArmor and SELinux\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dropping capabilities and read only root\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>RuntimeClass and sandboxed runtimes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-shared-kernel-risk\">The shared kernel risk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A container is a process with namespaces and cgroups. A vulnerability in a system call can let a process escape to the host. Reducing the system calls and privileges available lowers that risk, and strong isolation helps for untrusted code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"seccomp\">Seccomp\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Seccomp filters system calls. Setting \u003ccode>seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault\u003c/code> applies the runtime default profile, which blocks several dangerous calls with little breakage. Custom profiles can be recorded from a test run and reviewed. Unconfined is the weakest setting and should not be used.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"apparmor-and-selinux\">AppArmor and SELinux\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>These mandatory access control systems restrict files and operations per process. Use the profiles provided by the runtime or write specific ones for sensitive workloads. Check that your nodes support them, as availability depends on the operating system.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capabilities-and-file-system\">Capabilities and file system\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Drop all Linux capabilities and add back only what is needed, run as non-root, disallow privilege escalation and set the root file system read only. Many exploits need a writable path or a capability that a normal service never uses.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sandboxed-runtimes\">Sandboxed runtimes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A RuntimeClass selects a runtime for a pod, for example gVisor or Kata Containers. They add a user space kernel or a lightweight virtual machine, which costs some performance and compatibility but gives much stronger isolation. Use them for untrusted code, multi tenant workloads or build jobs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"app&gt;sc;sc&gt;rt;rt&gt;k\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Sandboxing: seccomp, AppArmor and runtime classes\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Container process\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sc\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Seccomp and MAC\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rt\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Runtime class\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"k\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Host kernel\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Container process to Seccomp and MAC; 2, Seccomp and MAC to Runtime class; 3, Runtime class to Host kernel.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Container process \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Makes syscalls.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Over privileged.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sc\">\u003cdt>Seccomp and MAC \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Filters calls.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unconfined.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rt\">\u003cdt>Runtime class \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Selects sandbox.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Incompatible app.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"k\">\u003cdt>Host kernel \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shared resource.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Kernel flaw.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-seccomp-profile-that-broke-a-release\">Incident: A seccomp profile that broke a release\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A custom profile blocked a system call the new library used.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs api-5c8 | tail -2\nkubectl get pod api-5c8 -o jsonpath='{.spec.securityContext.seccompProfile}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs api-5c8 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Fatal: operation not permitted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pod api-5c8 -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.securityContext.seccompProfile}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;localhostProfile&quot;:&quot;profiles/api.json&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Localhost&quot;}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The profile had been recorded before a library upgrade and missed a new call. The team switched to RuntimeDefault for the release, re-recorded the custom profile in a test environment and added a test that runs the app with the profile in CI.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Leaving seccomp unconfined\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running privileged containers for convenience\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writable root file systems\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding all capabilities back\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sandbox runtimes without testing compatibility\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-shared-kernel-risk","text":"The shared kernel risk"},{"id":"seccomp","text":"Seccomp"},{"id":"apparmor-and-selinux","text":"AppArmor and SELinux"},{"id":"capabilities-and-file-system","text":"Capabilities and file system"},{"id":"sandboxed-runtimes","text":"Sandboxed runtimes"},{"id":"incident-a-seccomp-profile-that-broke-a-release","text":"Incident: A seccomp profile that broke a release"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Restrict syscalls with seccomp","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/security/seccomp/"},{"t":"Restrict a container with AppArmor","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tutorials/security/apparmor/"},{"t":"Runtime class","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/runtime-class/"}],"text":"Containers share the host kernel, so a flaw in the kernel can become an escape. Syscall filters and sandboxed runtimes shrink the exposed kernel surface for workloads that need it. What you will learn The shared kernel risk Seccomp profiles and RuntimeDefault AppArmor and SELinux Dropping capabilities and read only root RuntimeClass and sandboxed runtimes The shared kernel risk A container is a process with namespaces and cgroups. A vulnerability in a system call can let a process escape to the host. Reducing the system calls and privileges available lowers that risk, and strong isolation helps for untrusted code. Seccomp Seccomp filters system calls. Setting seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault applies the runtime default profile, which blocks several dangerous calls with little breakage. Custom profiles can be recorded from a test run and reviewed. Unconfined is the weakest setting and should not be used. AppArmor and SELinux These mandatory access control systems restrict files and operations per process. Use the profiles provided by the runtime or write specific ones for sensitive workloads. Check that your nodes support them, as availability depends on the operating system. Capabilities and file system Drop all Linux capabilities and add back only what is needed, run as non-root, disallow privilege escalation and set the root file system read only. Many exploits need a writable path or a capability that a normal service never uses. Sandboxed runtimes A RuntimeClass selects a runtime for a pod, for example gVisor or Kata Containers. They add a user space kernel or a lightweight virtual machine, which costs some performance and compatibility but gives much stronger isolation. Use them for untrusted code, multi tenant workloads or build jobs. Sandboxing: seccomp, AppArmor and runtime classes Flow in order: 1, Container process to Seccomp and MAC; 2, Seccomp and MAC to Runtime class; 3, Runtime class to Host kernel. Service Security Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Container process Service What it does: Makes syscalls. If it fails or is weak: Over privileged. Seccomp and MAC Security What it does: Filters calls. If it fails or is weak: Unconfined. Runtime class Step What it does: Selects sandbox. If it fails or is weak: Incompatible app. Host kernel Security What it does: Shared resource. If it fails or is weak: Kernel flaw. Incident: A seccomp profile that broke a release Incident A custom profile blocked a system call the new library used. $ kubectl logs api-5c8 | tail -2 Fatal: operation not permitted $ kubectl get pod api-5c8 -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.securityContext.seccompProfile}&#x27; {\"localhostProfile\":\"profiles/api.json\",\"type\":\"Localhost\"} (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The profile had been recorded before a library upgrade and missed a new call. The team switched to RuntimeDefault for the release, re-recorded the custom profile in a test environment and added a test that runs the app with the profile in CI. Common mistakes Leaving seccomp unconfined Running privileged containers for convenience Writable root file systems Adding all capabilities back Sandbox runtimes without testing compatibility"}});